1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,170
Make the attack a little faster. It is
a familiar request during development.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:10,030
Should we shorten the preparation, or the pause after impact?

3
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:13,890
Consider a jump in Mega Man 11 or an attack in Monster Hunter Rise.

4
00:00:14,180 --> 00:00:17,490
One action contains a beginning, a change, and an ending.

5
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:21,310
We will translate that feeling into a time scale measured in frames.

6
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:23,890
The games illustrate observable motion.

7
00:00:23,960 --> 00:00:27,310
We will verify exact numbers in an original frame-timing test.

8
00:00:27,460 --> 00:00:30,990
The number of drawings is not necessarily the game’s time scale.

9
00:00:31,667 --> 00:00:34,647
Let us bring the same question into a fighting
game.

10
00:00:34,847 --> 00:00:40,207
Ky swings his sword in Guilty Gear, the
opponent reacts, and Ky returns to a stance.

11
00:00:40,567 --> 00:00:42,907
The bright effects attract your attention, but
watch three moments separately.

12
00:00:43,207 --> 00:00:45,967
The start of the action, contact with the
opponent,

13
00:00:45,967 --> 00:00:48,047
and the transition to another action.

14
00:00:48,307 --> 00:00:53,247
The sword's swing and the full duration of the
attack may have different boundaries.

15
00:00:53,527 --> 00:00:57,847
Street Fighter's Ken also brings his body back
after a kick.

16
00:00:57,847 --> 00:01:02,527
When someone asks for a faster attack, ask
which interval felt slow.

17
00:01:03,007 --> 00:01:05,287
Shorter preparation changes the opponent's
reading time;

18
00:01:05,287 --> 00:01:08,747
shorter recovery changes the wait before
another choice.

19
00:01:09,167 --> 00:01:10,847
We observed normal-speed movement.

20
00:01:10,847 --> 00:01:14,147
Exact numbers require a defined basis, as in
our original test.

21
00:01:15,213 --> 00:01:17,423
Every frame count needs a reference rate.

22
00:01:17,593 --> 00:01:22,223
At sixty steps per second, twelve
frames represent zero point two seconds.

23
00:01:22,373 --> 00:01:26,323
At thirty steps per second, the same twelve
frames represent zero point four seconds.

24
00:01:26,533 --> 00:01:29,503
The count stays the same, but the elapsed time doubles.

25
00:01:29,793 --> 00:01:33,843
Our test therefore uses a fixed sixty-update-per-second rule.

26
00:01:33,973 --> 00:01:37,883
It displays elapsed time alongside a moving marker.

27
00:01:38,253 --> 00:01:41,658
Record the frame count and its reference
rate together in your design notes.

28
00:01:41,673 --> 00:01:45,463
Rendering and game-rule updates can run at different rates.

29
00:01:46,167 --> 00:01:48,727
Before assigning numbers, practice observing
different actions with the same basis.

30
00:01:48,967 --> 00:01:53,047
Ky creates a projectile in the air, descends,
and returns to his stance.

31
00:01:53,287 --> 00:01:57,587
His pose keeps changing while the glowing
projectile remains on screen.

32
00:01:57,947 --> 00:01:59,887
Using the projectile's disappearance as the
attack's end

33
00:01:59,887 --> 00:02:01,887
may give a different answer from the
character's recovery.

34
00:02:02,147 --> 00:02:05,987
In the next action, watch him slide along the
ground and then stand up.

35
00:02:06,227 --> 00:02:08,287
Projectile travel, character recovery,

36
00:02:08,287 --> 00:02:11,667
and one displayed video frame are different
things to observe.

37
00:02:11,687 --> 00:02:14,927
Write the observed beginning and end beside
the number.

38
00:02:14,927 --> 00:02:19,367
A shared update basis lets us compare how long
we wait for different attacks.

39
00:02:19,503 --> 00:02:23,487
This is why we converted twelve frames into
seconds in the earlier explanation.

40
00:02:24,423 --> 00:02:26,953
Now decide which part of an attack you are measuring.

41
00:02:27,583 --> 00:02:31,073
Our original test records input acceptance as time zero.

42
00:02:31,503 --> 00:02:34,053
The hit area begins after twelve time steps,

43
00:02:34,283 --> 00:02:37,993
stays active for three steps, then recovers for fifteen.

44
00:02:38,243 --> 00:02:40,913
The total is thirty steps, or zero point five seconds.

45
00:02:41,163 --> 00:02:43,873
If the request for speed concerns preparation,

46
00:02:44,183 --> 00:02:47,113
we can shorten that phase and keep the others unchanged.

47
00:02:47,503 --> 00:02:51,148
Record both boundaries so everyone measures the same interval.

48
00:02:51,163 --> 00:02:55,493
These prototype numbers do not
represent move data from another game.

49
00:02:56,417 --> 00:02:59,897
Now watch attacks that rise into the air or
move toward the opponent.

50
00:03:00,197 --> 00:03:04,777
Ky raises his leg, moves forward, attacks, and
returns to a standing posture.

51
00:03:04,897 --> 00:03:08,857
Movement alone does not prove that an attack
hitbox is active at that moment.

52
00:03:09,117 --> 00:03:13,677
An attacker may also keep moving after the
opponent has been hit.

53
00:03:13,837 --> 00:03:17,517
For an upward leap, observe the ascent
separately from the landing.

54
00:03:17,577 --> 00:03:20,457
A player who needs another action may be
waiting for that ending.

55
00:03:20,517 --> 00:03:23,957
In Ken's sequence of kicks, observe the
connection between actions.

56
00:03:23,957 --> 00:03:25,937
Long preparation, long recovery,

57
00:03:25,937 --> 00:03:29,437
and a transition into another action are
separate questions.

58
00:03:29,657 --> 00:03:33,037
The earlier diagram compared a separate change
to preparation time.

59
00:03:33,790 --> 00:03:36,700
Does adding drawings make the hit happen sooner?

60
00:03:37,110 --> 00:03:40,660
We changed only the poses in the same attack.

61
00:03:40,910 --> 00:03:44,980
One version holds fewer poses longer;
the other changes poses more often.

62
00:03:45,150 --> 00:03:48,720
Both hit areas begin at the same twelfth time step.

63
00:03:49,050 --> 00:03:52,520
Their visual smoothness differs, but the wait for the hit is equal.

64
00:03:52,710 --> 00:03:56,600
We distinguish the animation-pose clock from the hit-area clock.

65
00:03:56,790 --> 00:04:00,095
Before adding poses, decide which timing you want to change.

66
00:04:00,110 --> 00:04:04,360
The implementation and logs verify that
poses and collision timing are separate.

67
00:04:05,150 --> 00:04:10,490
Guilty Gear's Sol presents strong poses and
then moves into another pose.

68
00:04:10,750 --> 00:04:14,610
Clear silhouettes and abrupt changes in shape
contribute to the impression of movement.

69
00:04:14,910 --> 00:04:20,270
Counting pose changes alone does not tell us
when an attack was active.

70
00:04:20,610 --> 00:04:25,650
Watch how Ky's character, weapon, and
projectile effects change differently.

71
00:04:25,950 --> 00:04:29,670
Light and projectiles may move while the
character holds a pose.

72
00:04:29,950 --> 00:04:34,030
Assuming every visible change belongs to one
collision clock makes measurement confusing.

73
00:04:34,030 --> 00:04:37,350
When refining expression, decide which pose
should remain readable for longer.

74
00:04:37,870 --> 00:04:40,810
Changing the action's preparation time
requires a separate comparison.

75
00:04:41,370 --> 00:04:45,470
That is why our test kept collision onset
fixed while changing pose counts.

76
00:04:46,433 --> 00:04:49,923
Next, examine a brief pause at impact.

77
00:04:50,113 --> 00:04:54,723
In our test, only the combat clock
pauses for four steps after collision.

78
00:04:54,913 --> 00:04:57,023
The background time indicator keeps moving.

79
00:04:57,253 --> 00:04:59,298
The attack data still spans thirty combat steps,

80
00:04:59,313 --> 00:05:02,883
but the actual completion time also includes the pause.

81
00:05:03,093 --> 00:05:07,923
When observing a paused action, ask which clock the count refers to.

82
00:05:08,093 --> 00:05:11,123
This does not imply that every commercial game uses this method.

83
00:05:11,353 --> 00:05:15,763
It illustrates explicitly choosing what
pauses and what continues in our own game.

84
00:05:16,433 --> 00:05:20,093
Focus on contact with the opponent and the
movement immediately afterward.

85
00:05:20,413 --> 00:05:24,933
Ky's sequence shows effects at contact before
connecting to another attack.

86
00:05:25,193 --> 00:05:28,033
Sol's strong hit also has different
impressions before contact,

87
00:05:28,033 --> 00:05:30,273
at impact, and when movement resumes.

88
00:05:30,413 --> 00:05:33,893
That brief feeling of impact is a different
question from the attack's preparation.

89
00:05:34,113 --> 00:05:38,733
Distinguish large effects from moments when
the character stops moving.

90
00:05:39,093 --> 00:05:41,733
A moving background or effect does not prove
that

91
00:05:41,733 --> 00:05:43,813
every combat-related clock keeps advancing.

92
00:05:43,813 --> 00:05:48,353
Game state and records are needed to establish
exactly what was paused.

93
00:05:48,653 --> 00:05:53,113
Our test paused only the combat clock and left
background time visible.

94
00:05:53,453 --> 00:05:57,493
We turn a question from real gameplay into a
small experiment with a defined subject.

95
00:05:58,367 --> 00:06:02,637
Finally, frames counted in a recording
are only one kind of evidence.

96
00:06:02,827 --> 00:06:06,817
Repeated or missing pictures can obscure the game’s internal timing.

97
00:06:07,087 --> 00:06:09,812
Even at different display update rates,

98
00:06:09,827 --> 00:06:13,637
the same fixed hit-area clock produces
the hit at the same elapsed time.

99
00:06:13,807 --> 00:06:16,637
First, watch at normal speed and estimate the duration.

100
00:06:16,827 --> 00:06:21,012
Then record input acceptance, the first active
hit, and when another action is allowed.

101
00:06:21,027 --> 00:06:23,492
Check the reference rate alongside the measured elapsed time

102
00:06:23,507 --> 00:06:26,872
to turn a vague request for speed into a change everyone can review.

103
00:06:26,887 --> 00:06:29,877
Find it by feel, then verify it by measurement.

104
00:06:29,967 --> 00:06:33,267
Finally, turn the observed attack into a
design note.

105
00:06:33,527 --> 00:06:37,147
For a projectile attack, separate hand
movement,

106
00:06:37,147 --> 00:06:39,567
projectile creation, and the return to a
stance.

107
00:06:39,807 --> 00:06:44,127
Record impact as another event because
distance also affects it.

108
00:06:44,287 --> 00:06:47,527
For a rushing attack, separate the start of
movement,

109
00:06:47,527 --> 00:06:49,587
contact, and readiness for another action.

110
00:06:49,847 --> 00:06:52,987
Then choose one interval to change in our own
game.

111
00:06:53,267 --> 00:06:55,627
If you change preparation, keep recovery and
travel

112
00:06:55,627 --> 00:06:57,967
distance fixed and test the same situation
again.

113
00:06:57,967 --> 00:07:02,807
Video checks visible results; logs check the
timing of internal rules.

114
00:07:03,027 --> 00:07:05,267
If the records differ, check whether frames
were

115
00:07:05,267 --> 00:07:07,567
miscounted or different clocks were observed.

116
00:07:07,827 --> 00:07:12,427
Keep the feeling, find where it arises, and
connect it to a change we can explain.
